import GroupApproximation.GGT.ElementaryClosure
import GroupApproximation.GGT.WPDHyperbolicallyEmbedded

/-!
# `E(g)` and hyperbolic embedding: the seam, and why clause (b) is base-dependent

Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin, Theorem 6.8: a loxodromic WPD element lies in a
hyperbolically embedded virtually cyclic subgroup, namely its elementary
closure.  Against `GGT.WPDHyperbolicallyEmbedded`'s vocabulary the target
`IsHypEmbeddedOf G Y (elementaryClosure g)` unpacks into a choice of relative
generating set `D` with `D.base = Y`, `D.fam = fun _ => elementaryClosure g`,
and the two fields of `IsHyperbolicallyEmbedded`:

* clause (a) `hyperbolic`    --- `Γ(G, Y ⊔ E(g))` is hyperbolic;
* clause (b) `locallyFinite` --- every relative ball `D.relBall () n` is finite.

This module is the seam: the assembly of the two clauses, the elementary facts
about `E(g)` that either half needs, and one theorem about what clause (b) can
and cannot be.

**The work split this module originally proposed is superseded, and the reason
is worth recording.**  It gave clause (a) to one agent and clause (b) to
another, as if a relative generating set were given in advance and hyperbolicity
of its cone-off were then an independent obligation.  That is not how the source
proceeds.  Theorem 4.42 (p.56) *produces* the relative generating set:

> Then there exists a relative generating set `X` of `G` with respect to
> `{H_λ}` and a constant `α > 0` such that the Cayley graph `Γ(G, X ⊔ H)` is
> hyperbolic, and for every `λ` and `h ∈ H_λ` we have `d̂_λ(1,h) ≥ α d(s,h(s))`.

So `X`, the hyperbolicity of `Γ(G, X ⊔ H)`, and the lower bound (41) all come
out of one construction --- projection complexes and a refined Milnor--Švarc
argument --- and clause (a) cannot be attacked before that construction exists.
The live split is the one in `GGT.ElementaryProjectionCriterion`: Theorem 4.42
is the single citation, and what divides into independent pieces is its three
*hypotheses* about `E(h)` --- quasiconvexity of an orbit, geometric separation,
and properness of the action.  Those are statements about the subgroup and the
action, not about a constructed alphabet, and they can be attacked in parallel.

The theorems below remain correct and useful --- `isHypEmbeddedOf_of_clauses` is
sound plumbing for any `D` however obtained --- but they are not a division of
labour.

## Clause (b) cannot be stated without the base, and that is a theorem

The tempting shape for a named input --- "for every `D` with
`D.fam = fun _ => E(g)`, the relative balls are finite" --- is **false**, and
`not_relBall_finite_of_subset_base` below refutes it rather than warning about
it.  If `E(g) ⊆ D.base` then every `h ∈ E(g)` is spelled by the one-letter word
`RelLetter.base h`, which is admissible and traverses no edge of `Γ_{E(g)}` ---
a base letter never does, whatever its endpoints --- so the whole of `E(g)` lies
in `D.relBall () 1`, which is infinite because `g` is loxodromic.

So the content of clause (b) is inseparable from the choice of base, and any
named input for it must carry that choice.  This is the same trap that
`RelGenSet.not_isHyperbolicallyEmbedded_of_fam_subset_base` closes at the level
of the whole structure; the point of proving it again at the level of
`relBall` is that it constrains the *statement* of the residual, not only the
choice of `D` at the end.

## What is proved here

* `infinite_elementaryClosure_of_isLoxodromic` --- `E(g)` is infinite, since it
  contains the infinite cyclic group generated by the loxodromic `g`.
* `not_isHypEmbeddedOf_subset_base` --- consequently `E(g)` is never
  hyperbolically embedded relative to a base containing it.
* `subset_relBall_one_of_subset_base` and `not_relBall_finite_of_subset_base`
  --- the base-dependence above.
* `dist_smul_listVal_le` --- the displacement of the element spelled by a word
  is at most the sum of the displacements of its letters.  This is the bridge
  every argument from a word bound to a `pairStab` membership crosses, and it is
  where clause (b) will meet `IsWPDAt`: an element of a relative ball spelled by
  a short word whose letters move the basepoint boundedly moves the basepoint
  boundedly, and `IsWPDAt` bounds how many elements can do that at two far-apart
  points of the axis.
* `isHypEmbeddedOf_of_clauses` --- the assembly, so that the two halves of the
  split compose without either side owning the choice of `D`.

## What is not proved here

Clause (b) itself.  The missing content is Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin's §4
estimate turning "spelled by a short `E(g)`-avoiding word" into "moves two
far-apart points of the axis of `g` a bounded amount", for a base chosen so that
its letters have bounded displacement.  `dist_smul_listVal_le` reduces that to a
bound on the letters, and the avoiding hypothesis is what has to supply the
bound for the `E(g)`-letters, which have no bounded displacement on their own ---
`E(g)` contains every power of `g`.  That is the whole difficulty and it is not
finessed here.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace GGT
namespace Elementary

open GroupApproximation.HullGeometry
open GroupApproximation.Manuscript.NonMF.TorsionFree

universe u v

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v} [PseudoMetricSpace X] [MulAction G X]

/-! ## `E(g)` is infinite -/

/-- The elementary closure of a loxodromic element is infinite: it contains the
cyclic subgroup generated by that element, which is infinite because a
loxodromic element has infinite order. -/
theorem infinite_elementaryClosure_of_isLoxodromic {g : G} {x : X}
    (hg : IsLoxodromic g x) :
    ((elementaryClosure g : Subgroup G) : Set G).Infinite := by
  intro hfin
  exact infinite_coe_zpowers hg
    (Set.Finite.subset hfin (fun y hy => zpowers_le_elementaryClosure g hy))

/-- **`E(g)` is never hyperbolically embedded relative to a base containing
it.**  The single-subgroup hygiene constraint, specialised to the elementary
closure of a loxodromic element, where infinitude is automatic. -/
theorem not_isHypEmbeddedOf_subset_base {g : G} {x : X} (hg : IsLoxodromic g x)
    {Y : Set G} (hsub : ((elementaryClosure g : Subgroup G) : Set G) ⊆ Y) :
    ¬ IsHypEmbeddedOf G Y (elementaryClosure g) :=
  not_isHypEmbeddedOf_of_subset hsub
    (infinite_elementaryClosure_of_isLoxodromic hg)

/-! ## Clause (b) is base-dependent -/

/-- **A base containing `E(g)` puts all of `E(g)` in the unit relative ball.**
For `h ∈ E(g) ⊆ D.base` the one-letter word `RelLetter.base h` is admissible,
spells `h`, and avoids `Γ_{E(g)}` --- a base letter is never a component letter,
so the avoidance condition is vacuous at it. -/
theorem subset_relBall_one_of_subset_base {g : G} (D : RelGenSet G Unit)
    (hfam : D.fam = fun _ => elementaryClosure g)
    (hbase : ((elementaryClosure g : Subgroup G) : Set G) ⊆ D.base) :
    ((elementaryClosure g : Subgroup G) : Set G) ⊆ D.relBall () 1 := by
  intro h hh
  refine RelGenSet.mem_relBall.mpr ⟨?_, [RelLetter.base h], ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · have hfam' : D.fam () = elementaryClosure g := congrFun hfam ()
    rw [hfam']
    exact hh
  · intro a ha
    rw [List.mem_singleton] at ha
    subst ha
    exact hbase hh
  · simp [RelLetter.listVal, RelLetter.val]
  · refine ⟨?_, trivial⟩
    rintro ⟨hcomp, -⟩
    exact hcomp
  · simp

/-- **Clause (b) is false for a base containing the family.**  So no named
residual for clause (b) can quantify over every relative generating set with the
right family; the choice of base is part of its content. -/
theorem not_relBall_finite_of_subset_base {g : G} {x : X}
    (hg : IsLoxodromic g x) (D : RelGenSet G Unit)
    (hfam : D.fam = fun _ => elementaryClosure g)
    (hbase : ((elementaryClosure g : Subgroup G) : Set G) ⊆ D.base) :
    ¬ (D.relBall () 1).Finite := by
  intro hfin
  exact infinite_elementaryClosure_of_isLoxodromic hg
    (Set.Finite.subset hfin (subset_relBall_one_of_subset_base D hfam hbase))

/-! ## From a word to a displacement -/

/-- **The displacement of the element a word spells is at most the sum of the
displacements of its letters.**

This is the bridge from a bound on relative word length to a `pairStab`
membership, which is the form `IsWPDAt` consumes.  It holds for any word over
any relative alphabet, with no avoidance hypothesis; the avoidance hypothesis is
what has to bound the summands coming from component letters. -/
theorem dist_smul_listVal_le (hiso : IsIsometricAction G X) (x : X)
    (w : List (RelLetter G Unit)) :
    dist x ((RelLetter.listVal w) • x)
      ≤ (w.map (fun a => dist x (a.val • x))).sum := by
  induction w with
  | nil =>
      rw [RelLetter.listVal_nil, one_smul, dist_self]
      simp
  | cons a t ih =>
      have hval : RelLetter.listVal (a :: t) = a.val * RelLetter.listVal t := by
        simp [RelLetter.listVal]
      have hstep : dist (a.val • x) ((a.val * RelLetter.listVal t) • x)
          = dist x ((RelLetter.listVal t) • x) := by
        rw [mul_smul]
        exact hiso a.val x ((RelLetter.listVal t) • x)
      rw [hval]
      refine le_trans
        (dist_triangle x (a.val • x) ((a.val * RelLetter.listVal t) • x)) ?_
      rw [hstep]
      simp only [List.map_cons, List.sum_cons]
      linarith

/-- A word all of whose letters move the basepoint by at most `C` moves it by at
most `C` times the length of the word. -/
theorem dist_smul_listVal_le_length_mul (hiso : IsIsometricAction G X) (x : X)
    {C : ℝ} (w : List (RelLetter G Unit))
    (hlet : ∀ a ∈ w, dist x (a.val • x) ≤ C) :
    dist x ((RelLetter.listVal w) • x) ≤ (w.length : ℝ) * C := by
  refine le_trans (dist_smul_listVal_le hiso x w) ?_
  have hsum : ∀ v : List (RelLetter G Unit),
      (∀ a ∈ v, dist x (a.val • x) ≤ C) →
        (v.map (fun a => dist x (a.val • x))).sum ≤ (v.length : ℝ) * C := by
    intro v
    induction v with
    | nil =>
        intro _
        simp
    | cons a t ih =>
        intro hcons
        have hhead : dist x (a.val • x) ≤ C := hcons a List.mem_cons_self
        have htail := ih (fun b hb => hcons b (List.mem_cons_of_mem a hb))
        simp only [List.map_cons, List.sum_cons, List.length_cons]
        push_cast
        linarith
  exact hsum w hlet

/-! ## The assembly -/

/-- **The two clauses compose.**  Given a relative generating set whose family
is `E(g)`, clause (a) and clause (b) for that same set give
`E(g) ↪_h (G, D.base)`.

Stated so that the two halves of the split can be attacked independently:
neither half has to own the choice of `D`, and whoever supplies `D` supplies it
once. -/
theorem isHypEmbeddedOf_of_clauses {g : G} (D : RelGenSet G Unit)
    (hfam : D.fam = fun _ => elementaryClosure g)
    (hhyp : ∃ δ : ℝ, IsHyperbolicSpace δ (Cayley D.alphabet))
    (hfin : ∀ n : ℕ, (D.relBall () n).Finite) :
    IsHypEmbeddedOf G D.base (elementaryClosure g) :=
  ⟨D, rfl, hfam, ⟨hhyp, fun _ n => hfin n⟩⟩

/-- The same, delivering `IsHypEmbedded` --- the form `DGOTheorem68` asks for,
where the base is existentially quantified. -/
theorem isHypEmbedded_of_clauses {g : G} (D : RelGenSet G Unit)
    (hfam : D.fam = fun _ => elementaryClosure g)
    (hhyp : ∃ δ : ℝ, IsHyperbolicSpace δ (Cayley D.alphabet))
    (hfin : ∀ n : ℕ, (D.relBall () n).Finite) :
    IsHypEmbedded G (elementaryClosure g) :=
  (isHypEmbeddedOf_of_clauses D hfam hhyp hfin).isHypEmbedded

end Elementary
end GGT
end GroupApproximation
